<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Marca Hispanica</h1><p class="st-meta">795 CE – 987 CE (approx.) · capital Barcelona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Carolingian frontier zone south and east of the Pyrenees, the cradle of the Catalan counties and of Catalonia itself.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After failing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 778, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne's</a> forces built a buffer zone against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> submitted in 785, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 801. The resulting counties, among them Barcelona, Girona, Urgell, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a>, Osona, Empúries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a>, became known as the Spanish March.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>It was never a single state but a group of counties under counts appointed by the Frankish kings, sometimes grouped under one marquis such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-septimania">Bernard of Septimania</a>. The Gothic population kept the Visigothic law code. Revolts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisso">Aissó's</a> in 826 and Muslim raids made it unstable. From 878 the counts, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wilfred the Hairy</a>, made their offices hereditary.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Frankish authority faded through the 10th century. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate's</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 985 no help came, and in 988 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borrell-ii-of-barcelona">Borrell II</a> did not renew homage to the new Capetian king, a step later seen as the birth of Catalan independence.</p></div>